.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:2:name: rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:3:description: Planning Task 03 incorrectly described the git checkout rollback path; corrected to git rm per the Ideation rollback contract.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:10:tags: [git-workflow, rollback, planning-correction]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:15:# Rollback semantics correction: git rm (not git checkout) after failed promote-now commit
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:19:The Planning brief for Task 03 (Preparation promote-now commit-on-branch) initially described the rollback sequence for a failed `git commit` post-copy as "restoration via git checkout, no auto-rm of skill body." This was wrong: the copied skill file did not pre-exist in the worktree, so `git checkout` has nothing to restore. The correct action is `git rm` to remove the copied file.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:21:The Ideation rollback contract (Design Decision D-3, "Partial-failure rollback") explicitly requires: if `git commit` fails post-copy, the manager MUST `git -C "$worktreePath" rm <copied-paths>` to remove the copied skill body before surfacing the failure to the user via AskUserQuestion.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:25:The Planning brief was corrected during Planning review to align with the Ideation rollback contract. The rollback sequence is now:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:26:1. `git commit` fails post-copy
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:27:2. Manager runs `git -C "$worktreePath" rm <copied-paths>` — REMOVES the copied file (not `git checkout`)
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:28:3. AskUserQuestion surfaces the failure to the user
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:33:The copied file did not pre-exist in the worktree before the promote-now operation. A `git checkout` would either fail (no prior version) or restore to HEAD (which also has no version of this file). The only correct recovery is removal.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:37:- **`git checkout <copied-paths>` (the original, incorrect brief wording)** — rejected: there is no prior version of the copied file to restore. `git checkout` either errors out (no tracked version) or restores to HEAD, which also has no version of the file, leaving the stray copy in place. It cannot undo the copy.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:38:- **Leave the copied file in place and surface the commit failure** — rejected: a half-promoted skill body left in the worktree is the partial-promotion failure the rollback contract exists to prevent; the worktree would carry an uncommitted, orphaned file.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:42:The verification spec for the Preparation promote-now implementation now includes grep gates confirming `git -C "$worktreePath" rm` and AskUserQuestion co-location in the shipped Preparation promote-now text. Any executor implementing the promote-now rollback path must use `git rm` to remove the copied file, not `git checkout`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:46:- `design/promote-now-commit-on-branch.md` — the commit-on-branch design (D-3) whose partial-failure rollback this decision corrects.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md:47:- `discussions/2026-05-24-promote-now-rollback-doc-home.md` — the discussion that placed this rollback's documentation home in `preparation/SKILL.md`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:2:name: direct-mode-retained-opt-out
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:3:description: Direct mode is preserved as a documented opt-out from worktree-first; users set git.workflow.mode=direct to bypass worktree creation.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:10:tags: [git-workflow, direct-mode, worktree-first, opt-out]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:18:Worktree-first makes every session boot on a worktree branch. The open question was whether worktree-first should be a hard mandate (every session, no exceptions) or a default with an escape hatch. Some sessions — emergency hotfixes, pure read-only investigations — have no need for an isolated worktree, and the prior `direct` workflow mode already supported running directly on the main tree. A mandate would have to either delete direct mode or override it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:22:The worktree creation step in the Configuration procedure carries a guard: `skip if session.json.git.workflow.mode == "direct"`. Worktree-first is locked as the **default**, not a **mandate** — the user can set `settings.git.workflow.mode = "direct"` to bypass worktree creation, leaving `worktreePath` null and the session running on the main tree. Direct mode is thus preserved as a documented escape hatch rather than removed.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:26:Steel-man analysis of the worktree-first mandate surfaced "emergency hotfix / pure read-only" sessions as legitimate use cases for direct mode. Removing direct mode would be a scope expansion beyond the user-locked decision; the user confirmed uniform worktree-first as the default *while preserving the opt-out*. Keeping the existing `direct` mode as the bypass costs nothing — it is already implemented — and avoids forcing worktree overhead onto sessions that do not benefit from it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:30:- **Make worktree-first a hard mandate and remove direct mode** — rejected: scope expansion beyond what the user locked, and it strands legitimate emergency-hotfix / read-only sessions that have no need for an isolated worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:31:- **Make worktree-first a mandate but keep direct mode dormant/undocumented** — rejected: an undocumented bypass is a trap; if the opt-out exists it must be discoverable at the step it opts out of.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:35:The worktree creation step in `orchestration/SKILL.md` Configuration Step 1 must carry the `skip if … mode == "direct"` guard (no code change beyond confirming the guard is present). `orchestration/SKILL.md` and `git/SKILL.md` must reference `settings.git.workflow.mode` identically — grep both files for `workflow.mode` to confirm matching references. The direct-mode opt-out text is documented at the worktree-creation step footnote (see `discussions/2026-05-24-direct-mode-opt-out-doc-home.md`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:39:- `discussions/non-feature-session-scope.md` — the discussion that locked uniform worktree-first with direct mode as the opt-out.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/direct-mode-retained-opt-out.md:40:- `discussions/2026-05-24-direct-mode-opt-out-doc-home.md` — where the opt-out text is documented.
