.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/promote-now-commit-on-branch.md:31:**Partial-failure rollback**: if `git commit` fails post-copy, manager MUST `git -C "$worktreePath" rm` the copied file and surface to user via AskUserQuestion before re-attempt.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/promote-now-commit-on-branch.md:35:The Preparation promote-now path (narrow exception allowing skill generation during Preparation) was already a sole-writer deviation; commit-on-branch is the structural completion that ties the generated artifact into worktree-first history. The `-C "$worktreePath"` flag is explicit about which tree receives the commit. The canonical `AI-Provenance-Record` trailer form is `gobbi://session/{session-id}/task/{task-id}` per `git/conventions.md`. The rollback mechanism (git rm after failed commit) addresses the partial-promotion failure mode where a copied file must be removed if the commit cannot complete.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/promote-now-commit-on-branch.md:40:- **An alternative `AI-Provenance-Record` trailer form** — rejected: a new trailer shape would require a separate Scope Contract item; the current `gobbi://session/{session-id}/task/{task-id}` slot is expressive enough to identify the promote-now operation.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/features/git-workflow/design/promote-now-commit-on-branch.md:44:The promote-now path now ends in a worktree-branch commit, tying the generated skill into worktree-first history. Any executor implementing this path must include the partial-failure rollback (`git rm` the copied file on commit failure, then AskUserQuestion) — see `decisions/2026-05-24-rollback-semantics-drift-from-ideation.md` for why `git rm` (not `git checkout`) is the only correct recovery. Verification: a future-session smoke test confirms `git log --oneline` for the next `generate-now` shows a subject matching `^chore\(skills\): promote .*` with a body containing `AI-Provenance-Record: gobbi://session/[a-f0-9-]+/task/preparation-promote-now-iter[0-9]+`.
